<header><p class="st-kicker">other · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Royal Niger Company territories</h1><p class="st-meta">1886 CE – 1900 CE · capital Asaba</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The lands along the Niger and Benue ruled by George Goldie's chartered company, which secured the region for Britain against French and German rivals.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-taubman-goldie">George Taubman Goldie</a> merged rival British trading firms on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-niger-company">United African Company</a> in 1879, renamed the National African Company in 1882. He bought out French competitors, and his agents signed hundreds of treaties with local rulers. In 1886 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> granted the firm a royal charter as the Royal Niger Company, with power to govern the lower Niger and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benue-river">Benue</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The company used its charter to enforce a trade monopoly, charging heavy duties that ruined African middlemen such as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nembe-kingdom">Nembe</a> traders of Brass, who attacked its depot at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akassa-raid">Akassa</a> in 1895. It kept its own constabulary, administrative centre at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asaba">Asaba</a> and trading posts up the rivers. In 1897 its forces defeated the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bida-emirate">Bida Emirate</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ilorin-emirate">Ilorin Emirate</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Criticism of the monopoly and the need for state forces against French expansion led <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> to revoke the charter on 1 January 1900. The territories passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">Protectorates</a> of Northern and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/protectorate-of-southern-nigeria">Southern Nigeria</a>, and the company continued as a trading business.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/royal-niger-company-territories.jpg" alt="Royal Niger Company territories" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Bohr · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Royal_Niger_Company_flag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1886 CE – 1900 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Asaba</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>George Taubman Goldie</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Islam, traditional religion, Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English, Hausa, Igbo, Nupe</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/nigeria">Nigeria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Royal Niger Company, National African Company territories</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1893&amp;focus=royal-niger-company-territories" aria-label="Royal Niger Company territories on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4867 1946 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Royal Niger Company territories lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5187 2202L5127 2049M5187 2202L5188 2204M5187 2202L5186 2169M5187 2202L4997 651M5187 2202L5170 2068M5187 2202L5126 2116M5187 2202L5166 2094M5187 2202L5181 2226"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5127 2049h0M5188 2204h0M5186 2169h0M4997 651h0M5170 2068h0M5126 2116h0M5166 2094h0M5181 2226h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5187 2202h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5187 2202h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Asaba. <a href="/atlas?year=1893&amp;focus=royal-niger-company-territories">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
